# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Lecture 45 objective pool B -- Acid-Base Disorders II (Finck): mixed disorders (s23, s26-30: the rules, the six
mixed patterns of s28-30 with their keys and paired clinical examples) and the Winter formula with the change in
anion gap against the change in bicarbonate (s27, s49-50).

KEYS ARE WRITTEN SHORT ON PURPOSE -- detail lives in the explanation.
EXPLANATIONS follow tools/question_writing/EXPLAIN_DEEP.md: the key names the decisive clue and the fact; each wrong
choice says why it fails here, the replacing fact, and a tell.

Set 1 is recall and interpretation against the syllabus objectives, so no stem carries a patient. The Winter formula
and the gap formula are never given (expected known); the accepted values 10 and 25 are named where an item needs
them, as s27/s50 state them. Every Winter value pairs a bicarbonate and a carbon dioxide where the s24 1.3 rule
(from 24 or 25) reaches the same conclusion. No item asks the sign of the change in bicarbonate, computes a change in
chloride (the deck gives no baseline chloride), or uses the s28-30 example values themselves (the s30 row 1 example,
bicarbonate 25 with carbon dioxide 40, sits below its own Winter range, so it is never reused). Metabolic alkalosis
items give the s24 0.75 ratio in the stem.
"""
